Let $A$ be a finite alphabet and $A^*$ the free monoid of finite words over $A$. Given a family $F \subseteq A^*$ of nonempty words, an $F$-factorization of a word $w \in A^*$ is a factorization $w = u_1 u_2 \cdots u_k$ with each $u_i \in F$. The factorization is called regular when $F$ is a regular language, and we call it a finite-language $F$-factorization when $F$ is a finite language.

Consider the standard algorithmic problems associated with $F$-factorizations, such as:
(i) deciding, given $w \in A^*$, whether $w$ admits an $F$-factorization;
(ii) counting the number of distinct $F$-factorizations of $w$;
(iii) producing one (or all) $F$-factorization(s) of $w$;
(iv) computing an optimal $F$-factorization with respect to a natural cost (e.g.\ minimum or maximum number of factors).

The benchmark complexities are those obtained when $F$ is allowed to be an arbitrary regular language, given by a finite automaton or regular expression of size $m$, with input word length $n$.

Question: Can asymptotically faster algorithms (in $n$ and in the size of the description of $F$) be designed for problems (i)–(iv) under the restriction that $F$ is a finite language, compared with the best known algorithms in the general regular case?

